        <h2 class="subhead" id="mini-regex">Overview</h1>
            
            <p>
                As part of the FPGA handwritten digit recognition project, dynamic resizing of the HDMI input video stream needed to be done. This included cropping the 
                720x1280p input video stream and resizing that sub-region into a 28x28 image.
            </p>
            <p>
               &#60 Image of high level I/O &#62
            </p>
            <p>
            Main performance metrics:
                <ul>
                    <li>Gate count</li>
                    <li>Register count</li>
                    <li>Latency</li>
                </ul>
            </p><br>

        <h2 class="subhead" id="algorithim">Algorithim</h1>
            <p>
                This algorithim needs to allow for upsampling and downsampling of the input image on both the horrizontal and vertical axis. This will also
                ideally require no change in logic to switch between these modes.
            </p>
            <p>
                The algorithim that was choosen was to translate the coordinate system from the source image to the destination image. The new destination pixels 
                will get a value according to a weighted average of all the source pixels that it overlaps.
            </p>

            <p>
                We are given the source_frame[w][h] array and would like to produce the destination_frame[28][28] array. <br>

                This can then be interpreted as the below continuous function. <br>

                source_continous(x, y) = source_frame[floor(x)][floor(y)] |  {0 <= x < w, 0 <= y <= h} <br>

                where x and y are floating point variables. <br>

                Given a output coordinate i and j, this will occupy an area of between i -> i + 1 and j -> j + 1

                This box can be translated to the source coordinate space by changing the bases:

                x_dest = 28*x_src/w; x_src = w*x_dest/28
                y_dest = 28*y_src/h; y_src = h*y_dest/28

                Given the source floating point co-ordinate box, the weighted average of the pixels in that area can be take.

                This will be equivilent to the following:



                Now we would like to solve for dest_descrete[i][j], where 0 <= i, j < 28.

                for each output pixel value we can determine the continous coordinate bounds in the destination domain.
                
                top_left_dest = (i, j)
                top_right_dest = (i + 1, j + 1)

                The same coordinates in the source domain can be found by translating the values.

                we can then get the average value over this domain to get the destination value.


                intergral(source_continous) in domain top_left_dest -> top_right_dest / size of source area

                destination_frame[i][j] = translated(i, j)


            </p>

            <p>
                This will be equivilent to the following:
                $$ dest\_array[i][j] = \frac{\int_{A} f(x, y) dxdy}{\int_{A} dxdy}$$

                Where A is the source domain box. And f(x, y) is the continous source function.

            </p>
            <p>
                Given that the image may be upsampled or downsampled, this area A may be less than 1 (upsapling) or greater than 1 (downsampling)
            </p>

        <h2 class="subhead" id="mini-regex">Algorithim</h1>

       <p>For use on ASCII strings. Supported special characters:</p>
        <table>
        <thead>
        <tr>
        <th>Character</th>
        <th>Meaning</th>
        </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody><tr>
        <td>|</td>
        <td>OR two possible matches</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
        <td>*</td>
        <td>Repeat the previous group 0 or more times</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
        <td>+</td>
        <td>Repeat the previous group 1 or more times</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
        <td>?</td>
        <td>Match to previous group or skip</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
        <td>( )</td>
        <td>Start grouping/end grouping</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
        <td>[]</td>
        <td>Match to a caracter set</td>
        </tr>
        </tbody></table>
        <h4 id="not-supported-right-now">Not supported right now:</h4>
        <ul>
        <li>Macros of common character sets (ie: \w \d ... ext)</li>
        <li>Repeat grouping N times operation {N}</li>
        <li>Inverse of character set [^x]</li>
        <li>Match to start or end of string ($ / ^)</li>
        </ul>
        <h4 id="character-sets">Character sets:</h4>
        <p>Character sets will take the inside of the brackets as a literal. If there is a dash (-) then the range between the two ASCII codes will be taken.</p>
